-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
help for ^nl potfun^                                                     
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Nonlinear least squares Alometric function estimation
-----------------------------------------------------

   ^nl potfun^ weightvar lengthvar [weight] [^if^ exp] [^in^ range]
              [^, l^evel^(^#^)^ ^i^nit^(^...^)^ ^ln^lsq^(^#^)^ ^lea^ve ^eps(^#^)^
              ^nolo^g ^tr^ace ^it^erate^(^#^)^ fcn_options ]


weightvar is the dependent variable with individual or mean body weight values.

lengthvar is the independent variable with a body length measure.

^aweights^ and ^fweight^s are allowed; see help ^weights^.

^nl potfun^ as being an application of ^nl^ shares the features of all estimation 
   commands; see help ^est^.


The syntax of ^predict^ following ^nl vbgfn^ is

   predict [type] newvarname [^if^ exp] [^in^ range] [, { ^y^hat | ^r^esiduals }]


^yhat^ and ^residuals^ are available both in and out of sample; type "^predict^ ...
^if e(sample)^ ..." if wanted only for the estimation sample.


Description
-----------

This command uses Stata's nl program to fit a potential Function
to the dependent variable which is body weight or mean weight by least squares. 
The potential function is in the separate program nlpotfun.ado. As an 
application of the nl procedure included in Stata, the ^nl potfun^ program 
shares all the options for ^nl^ (see help ^nl^).


Options
-------

^level(^#^)^ specifies the confidence level, in percent, for confidence intervals
    of the coefficients; see help ^level^.

^init(^...^)^ specifies initial values for parameters that are to be used to over-
    ride the default initial values.

^lnlsq(^#^)^ fits the model defined in fcn by 'log least squares', defined as with
    shifted lognormal errors. In other words, ln(depvar-#) is assumed normally
    distributed. Sums of squares and deviance are adjusted to the same scale as
    depvar.

^leave^ leaves behind after estimation a set of new variables with the same names
    as the estimated parameters containing the derivative of E(y) with respect
    to the parameter.

^eps(^#^)^ specifies the convergence criterion for successive parameter estimates
    and for the residual sum of squares. Default: 1e-5 (.00001).

^nolog^ suppresses the iteration log.

^trace^ expands the iteration log to provide more details, including values of
    the parameters at each step of the process.

^iterate(^#^)^ specifies the maximum number of iterations before giving up and
    defaults to 100.

fcn_options refer to any options allowed by nlfcn.


Options for ^predict^
-------------------

^yhat^, the default, calculates the fitted values.

^residuals^ calculates the residuals.


nlpotfun
--------

^nl potfun^ uses the function defined by nlvbgfn.  In nlpotfun the parameters of  
the function and the set of default initial values are determined, and it 
permit to evaluate the function for a given set of parameter estimates.

The nlpotfun is:

^program define nlpotfun
   version 6.0
      if "`1'"=="?"  {
         global S_2 /*
*/ "Potential function,$S_E_depv=b0*`2'^b1"
         global S_1 "b0 b1"
         local exp "[$S_E_wgt $S_E_exp]"
         global b0=.5
         global b1=.5
         exit
      }
      replace `1'= $b0*`2'^$b1
end^


Examples
--------

Having ^eviwei^ a variable with eviscerated weight at each ^bodlen^ (body length), 
you type:

^. nl potfun eviwei bodlen^

Notice that the initial values of the parameters are provided in the nlpotfun
program.  You can, however, override them.  To estimate a model using 3 
for the initial value of b0 (b) rather than 0.5, you can type

^. nl potfun eviwei bodlen, init(b0 = 3)^

To also change the initial value of b1 (a) from 0.5 to 1, you type:

^. nl potfun eviwei bodlen, init(b0 = 3, b1 = 1)^

